Apple truth:
People don’t trust claims.
They trust evidence presented calmly.
Your case study section should feel like:
A quiet briefing
A boardroom slide
A system postmortem (without the drama)
Not marketing. Not storytelling. Proof.
How Apple Thinks About Case Studies
Apple does not say:
“Here’s how creative we were”
Apple says:
“Here’s the problem. Here’s what we did. Here’s what changed.”
That’s the model we’ll use.
Case Study Section — High-Level Structure
Section title (locked)
Selected work
Optional subline (only one line):
A small sample of systems designed for reliability and scale.
Each Case Study Card (THIS IS THE SYSTEM)
Each case study must follow this exact order:
Client Context (who + environment)
Problem (what was at risk)
Solution (what you built or changed)
Outcome (measurable or concrete result)
No extra sections. No fluff.
Apple-Approved Case Study Template
Here’s the canonical format:
Client
Mid-sized enterprise operating in a regulated environment
Challenge
Legacy systems were unstable under load, creating operational and security risk.
Solution
We redesigned the system architecture and deployed a secure, scalable platform.
Outcome
Improved system reliability, reduced operational incidents, and enabled future growth.
That’s it.
Readable in 8–10 seconds on mobile.
How Many Case Studies?
Apple rule:
Fewer, stronger.
2–4 case studies max
If you have 1 strong one → that’s fine
Never add weak ones to “fill space”
Visual Design Rules (Very Important)
Subtle glass cards only
No screenshots unless they add meaning
No logos unless client-approved
Plenty of white space
Clear typographic hierarchy
This should feel heavier than the rest of the page — not visually, but psychologically.